Here is the problem description:
<problem_description>
This is the easy version of the problem. The only difference between the easy version and the hard version is the constraints on $$$n$$$. You can only make hacks if both versions are solved.A permutation of $$$1, 2, \ldots, n$$$ is a sequence of $$$n$$$ integers, where each integer from $$$1$$$ to $$$n$$$ appears exactly once. For example, $$$[2,3,1,4]$$$ is a permutation of $$$1, 2, 3, 4$$$, but $$$[1,4,2,2]$$$ isn't because $$$2$$$ appears twice in it.Recall that the number of inversions in a permutation $$$a_1, a_2, \ldots, a_n$$$ is the number of pairs of indices $$$(i, j)$$$ such that $$$i &lt; j$$$ and $$$a_i &gt; a_j$$$.Let $$$p$$$ and $$$q$$$ be two permutations of $$$1, 2, \ldots, n$$$. Find the number of permutation pairs $$$(p,q)$$$ that satisfy the following conditions:  $$$p$$$ is lexicographically smaller than $$$q$$$.  the number of inversions in $$$p$$$ is greater than the number of inversions in $$$q$$$. Print the number of such pairs modulo $$$mod$$$. Note that $$$mod$$$ may not be a prime.
</problem_description>

Here is the Java 11 code you have to repair:
<original_code>
import java.util.*;
import java.io.*;

public class e1542easy {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        int n = sc.nextInt();
        int mod = sc.nextInt();
        sc.close();
        solve(n, mod);
    }

    static void solve(int n, int mod) {
        List<int[]> inv = new ArrayList<>();
        inv.add(new int[]{1});

        for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
            int[] prev = inv.get(i - 1);
            int len = i * (i + 1) / 2 + 1;
            int[] curr = new int[len];

            for (int j = 0; j < len; j++) {
                int val = 0;
                int max = Math.min(j, i);
                for (int k = 0; k <= max; k++) {
                    int idx = j - k;
                    if (idx < prev.length) {
                        val = (val + prev[idx]) % mod;
                    }
                }
                curr[j] = val;
            }
            inv.add(curr);
        }

        int[] res = new int[n];
        res[0] = 0;

        for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
            long cur = (long) res[i - 1] * i % mod;
            int[] invs = inv.get(i - 1);

            for (int a = 0; a < invs.length; a++) {
                for (int b = a + 2; b < invs.length; b++) {
                    int d = b - a - 1;
                    long term = (long) invs[a] * invs[b] * d * (2L * i - d + 1) / 2;
                    cur = (cur + term) % mod;
                }
            }
            res[i] = (int) ((cur % mod + mod) % mod);
        }

        System.out.println(res[n - 1]);
    }
}
